[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  tuesday june 28th i'm julie chang for  the wall street journal filling in for  zoe thomas  so-called algorithmic stable coins are  supposed to keep at a value of one  dollar unlike regular stable coins which  are backed by actual funds or assets  algorithmic ones use other cryptos to  maintain their peg  but one stablecoin tara usd imploded  last month as the crypto sector as a  whole nosedived terra usd is currently  valued at pennies and the collapse of  terra usd and luna cost investors some  40 billion dollars  now the man who created the kryptos is  trying again  south korean entrepreneur dokwan has  relaunched luna the crypto that was  being used to keep terra usd pegged to a  dollar but what's different this time  around wsj reporter alexander osiepovich  has been following the story and he  joins us now hi alex thank you for  coming back on the show hi julie thanks  for having me  so can you start us off with a little  bit of background who is dokwan and what  is he like do kwan is a south korean  cryptocurrency entrepreneur  for a few years he was very vocal in  building out  a family of cryptocurrencies that all  had the names tara and luna involved in  them and he started a company called  terraform labs that was the main  company that developed projects related  to all of this dokwan uh was very  outspoken on twitter he talked a lot  about how his cryptocurrencies were  going to sort of take over the world and  was very good about drumming up business  basically at least until may when it all  collapsed  so we had you on the show before to help  cover algorithmic stable coins stable  coins or cryptos pegged to the us dollar  algorithmic stable coins used another  crypto to maintain their value instead  of actual dollars or other real assets  in this case it was luna  what went wrong with terra usd and luna  so the idea was if terra usd would drop  below a dollar you could make money by  swapping it for a dollars worth of luna  or if terra usd for some reason went  above a dollar you could swap a one  dollar worth of luna for you know a one  tara usd and make money that way  and that actually worked surprisingly  well for a long time in keeping terry  usd at the price of one dollar  but the whole mechanism was dependent on  the idea that people would actually want  luna that luna would have some value and  that people  would consider it sort of a legitimate  useful backing for terra usd  what happened in may was that during a  period of volatility in the crypto  markets tara usd lost its peg it dropped  below one dollar at first it would drop  down to you know 98 cents or so then it  dropped down to 91 90 cents they tried  to make it recover a couple of times  there was a reserve fund in place where  dokwan and his team essentially sold  bitcoin and tried to prop up the price  of tara usd but they burned through this  pretty fast and the whole thing  collapsed  now both terra usd and luna are  essentially worthless this wiped out  around 40 billion dollars worth of value  so then what's happened to dokwan since  luna crashed  do kwan is hanging tight  he has been facing an investigation in  his native south korea a lot of  investors complained that he had  committed fraud and illegal fundraising  and south korea prosecutors in seoul  told us that they were investigating  he is also facing a lawsuit in the  united states where an investor filed a  lawsuit in california seeking class  action status which made similar  allegations to what the south korean  investors said  terraform labs told us that they would  not comment on any active investigations  as far as a lawsuit goes  terraform labs dokwan's company told us  that would fight the lawsuit in court  they described it as meritless  we should know dokwan has denied that he  defrauded anyone that he had the support  of various big players in the crypto  industry and that he lost money in the  crash himself  and now in the last few weeks dokuan  came out with a new version of luna can  you tell us more about that and what  happens to people's old luna or terra  usds  yes so dokwan is essentially attempting  a reboot  he argues that the kind of system that  he created which is based on something  called the terra blockchain is useful  and has other good things about it  besides this algorithmic stablecoin  which crashed and it is worth noting  that the terra blockchain has these  other projects based on it sort of  lending and trading protocols kind of  like apps that you can build on top of  that operating system as a way to think  about it  so at the end of may they created a  brand new version of the terra  blockchain and they created a new  cryptocurrency which is also called luna  and people who held the old worthless  versions of luna and terra usd were  given the units of the new luna the old  luna was renamed luna classic  and people in sort of a way of  compensation sort of a way to get  activity going in this new blockchain  system  people were given units of the new luna  and there was this whole complex formula  that was behind this but basically they  decided to allocate the new version of  luna to people who had held the old  cryptocurrencies  using a sort of formula based on how  much you held before the crash how much  you held after the crash and so on  but the new luna it began training in  late may and it's already lost a lot of  its value right  yes uh it debuted at almost nineteen  dollars per coin and immediately  collapsed lately it's been trading  around two dollars per coin  basically it appears that a lot of  people were given this and immediately  dumped it and fled to some sort of safer  assets or maybe even to real money so  what do dokwan's critics say about him  launching a new luna  dokuan has a lot of critics people felt  that he did too aggressive a sales job  on his previous  project and essentially shield tara usd  and luna too hard  and they think it's kind of crazy that  he's trying to begin again there's  really nothing stopping him from doing  that in crypto it's an unregulated  market so he has just started over again  and is trying to drum up interest and in  your story you mentioned that dokwan is  a pretty polarizing figure which means  he also has his supporters so why do he  and his supporters think that this is  going to work a second time around  yes he has a community of supporters uh  they call themselves lunatics after the  luna cryptocurrency  and at least some of them are believers  and they think that luna will have a  second coming and they will be able to  make money as the new project rises  dokwan himself says that there is a  community of people who were built  around the terra blockchain people who  were developing different apps trading  on it and who were sort of bought into  its prospect as a technology platform  and he says that as long as there's more  activity on the new terra blockchain  eventually luna will rise again and  there will be other things on it so far  there is no plan to launch an  algorithmic stablecoin on the new  terra blockchain i think if people did  that then there would be some worries  about history repeating itself  all right that was our reporter  alexander ossopovich alex thank you  again for coming on the show thanks for  having me  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm julie chang for the wall street  journal thanks for listening  [Music]  you

